<edge source="energy_chokepoints::chokepoint_security" target="global_bond_rate_shock::market_liquidity" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">-</data>
  <data key="d29">2</data>
  <data key="d36">0.6</data>
  <data key="d37">Deterioration in chokepoint security raises counterparty and operational risk for commodity trading and shipping finance, triggering higher margin requirements and dealer risk aversion that reduce short-term fixed-income market liquidity.</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">energy_chokepoints</data>
  <data key="d40">global_bond_rate_shock</data>
</edge>
<edge source="energy_chokepoints::chokepoint_security" target="iran_regional_escalation::maritime_incident_rate" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">-</data>
  <data key="d29">3</data>
  <data key="d36">0.9</data>
  <data key="d37">A deterioration in chokepoint security lowers barriers to hostile acts (attacks, seizures, harassment) by state and non‑state actors, directly increasing the rate of disruptive maritime incidents.</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">energy_chokepoints</data>
  <data key="d40">iran_regional_escalation</data>
</edge>
<edge source="energy_chokepoints::chokepoint_security" target="iran_regional_escalation::military_engagement" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">-</data>
  <data key="d29">2</data>
  <data key="d36">0.75</data>
  <data key="d37">Sustained reductions in chokepoint security prompt regional and extra‑regional navies and air forces to increase patrols, escorts, interdictions, and targeted strikes to protect transits, raising the intensity of kinetic engagements.</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">energy_chokepoints</data>
  <data key="d40">iran_regional_escalation</data>
</edge>
<edge source="energy_chokepoints::chokepoint_security" target="russia_ukraine_war::escalation_pressure_inde" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">-</data>
  <data key="d29">2</data>
  <data key="d36">0.6</data>
  <data key="d37">An increase in chokepoint insecurity (more attacks or interdictions) provokes naval escorts, interdiction operations and political-military responses from states dependent on the routes, which raises incentives and pressure to widen or intensify military action in the conflict theatre.</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">energy_chokepoints</data>
  <data key="d40">russia_ukraine_war</data>
</edge>
<edge source="energy_chokepoints::chokepoint_security" target="us_china_tech_rivalry::national_security_pressu" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">+</data>
  <data key="d29">3</data>
  <data key="d36">0.8</data>
  <data key="d37">Acute insecurity or attacks at major maritime chokepoints produce immediate strategic alarm among US policymakers and military planners, prompting tighter export controls, sanctions, and scrutiny of China-linked AI supply chains as part of risk-mitigation measures.</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">energy_chokepoints</data>
  <data key="d40">us_china_tech_rivalry</data>
</edge>
