<edge source="us_china_tech_rivalry::national_security_pressu" target="energy_chokepoints::chokepoint_security" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">-</data>
  <data key="d29">3</data>
  <data key="d36">0.7</data>
  <data key="d37">Rising national-security pressure in the US–China tech rivalry prompts increased naval deployments, patrols and contested maneuvers around major maritime chokepoints (e.g., South China Sea approaches), raising the frequency of close encounters and transits disruptions and thereby reducing operational openness and security.</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">us_china_tech_rivalry</data>
  <data key="d40">energy_chokepoints</data>
</edge>
<edge source="us_china_tech_rivalry::national_security_pressu" target="energy_chokepoints::oil_spot_price" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">+</data>
  <data key="d29">3</data>
  <data key="d36">0.65</data>
  <data key="d37">Elevated security-driven measures (export controls, sanctions threats, contingency planning) increase perceived supply risk and induce immediate risk premia in physical markets, pushing up near-term oil spot prices.</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">us_china_tech_rivalry</data>
  <data key="d40">energy_chokepoints</data>
</edge>
<edge source="us_china_tech_rivalry::national_security_pressu" target="global_bond_rate_shock::market_liquidity" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">-</data>
  <data key="d29">3</data>
  <data key="d36">0.7</data>
  <data key="d37">Escalating security-driven export controls and de-risking between the U.S. and China fragment cross-border clearing, custody and dealer networks so dealers pull back inventories and widen bid-ask spreads, reducing short-term bond market liquidity.</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">us_china_tech_rivalry</data>
  <data key="d40">global_bond_rate_shock</data>
</edge>
<edge source="us_china_tech_rivalry::national_security_pressu" target="iran_regional_escalation::sanctions_enforcement" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">+</data>
  <data key="d29">3</data>
  <data key="d36">0.75</data>
  <data key="d37">Heightened national-security pressure drives faster implementation of export controls and enhanced intelligence-sharing among allies, producing more aggressive targeting, interdiction, and legal enforcement actions against networks linked to Iran.</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">us_china_tech_rivalry</data>
  <data key="d40">iran_regional_escalation</data>
</edge>
<edge source="us_china_tech_rivalry::national_security_pressu" target="iran_regional_escalation::oil_gas_flow" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">+</data>
  <data key="d29">2</data>
  <data key="d36">0.6</data>
  <data key="d37">If US-led tech pressure limits China's access to Western suppliers, China may increase energy purchases from Iran as a political/economic offset, directly raising Iranian oil/gas export volumes to China.</data>
  <data key="d38">6-18 months</data>
  <data key="d39">us_china_tech_rivalry</data>
  <data key="d40">iran_regional_escalation</data>
</edge>
<edge source="us_china_tech_rivalry::national_security_pressu" target="russia_ukraine_war::defense_funding_flow_rat" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">-</data>
  <data key="d29">2</data>
  <data key="d36">0.65</data>
  <data key="d37">Rising national-security pressure from the U.S.–China tech rivalry redirects political attention and budgetary priorities toward Indo‑Pacific capabilities, causing legislators and the executive to delay or reduce the rate of new Ukraine military aid appropriations.</data>
  <data key="d38">6-18 months</data>
  <data key="d39">us_china_tech_rivalry</data>
  <data key="d40">russia_ukraine_war</data>
</edge>
